                    ]
        description
            Build release versions on production: key fingerprints, identity versions, saved-shape resets with record
            migration, and `previous` holds. This Task owns every key's fate, quarantine's exits included (@spec.Records
            R13); @task.V3.T92 builds the codec and `admit`, which owns no state.

            **Why now:** an upgraded release must reset or keep each saved record correctly, and a saved shape is
            defined only once @task.V3.T17 and @task.V3.T57 fix private and computed fields. It is blocked by
            @task.V3.T124 because `resource({ persist: true })` writes a saved record format that this Task versions.

            **Read first:** @plan.Persistence.O5, @plan.Machines.O5 (paused), @convention.interaction-state. Spec:
            @spec.Records (R6–R8, R12, R13, R19, I4, I12, I13, I15, I17), companion `wiki/specs/v3/Records.md`;
            @spec.Schedules (R14, I11), companion `wiki/specs/v3/Schedules.md`.

            **Build-time choice (`codecSig`, @spec.Records Q4):** the fingerprint must be stable under module load order
            and a minified build. A: hash the Schema AST's encoded side, field names sorted. B: hash the declared names
            and kinds only. A also catches a changed field type; B survives more builds but misses type changes, which
            decode then catches anyway (the stamp never admits, R19). Test: spike A on the fixtures under two load
            orders and a minified build; if any stamp differs, take B. A miss costs a refused hydrate only.

            The linked Spec is pre-coding analysis: follow it as best-guess guidance for how to build, not a strict
            contract; it should help a lot. Where building shows it wrong, fix the Spec and log the change
            in `wiki/specs/specs.log`.

