    T117
        title       Fast tests again: the home-rooms stress sweep back under its bound
        status      draft
        priority    high
        parent      [@T136]

        description
            The `sweep-stress-land-0` shard runs under its bound again, by making schedule firings cheap or by a
            recorded re-measurement of the bound.

            **Why now:** the shard runs 193 s against its 164 s bound (measured at 41 s earlier); the tree before T57's
            last round already took 207 s. The profile points at schedule work and record writes on every firing in the
            home-rooms Stories. The Epic takes a Task to make the tests fast again before the next one.

            **Read first:** @task.V3.T57 (its last close note), code:packages/flow-state-v3/src/core/schedules.ts,
            @convention.bench. Spec: @spec.Host (R10, I18), companion `wiki/specs/v3/Host.md`; @spec.Schedules (R17,
            I18), companion `wiki/specs/v3/Schedules.md`.

            The linked Spec is pre-coding analysis: follow it as best-guess guidance for how to build, not a strict
            contract; it should help a lot. Where building shows it wrong, fix the Spec and log the change
            in `wiki/specs/specs.log`.

            **Supersedes:** none of its own; the cost falls with the replacements of @task.V3.T118 and @task.V3.T119,
            which it measures. Do not delete it before its replacement lands and the suite passes.

            May change: `packages/flow-state-v3/src/`, `tests/`, `fixtures/`, `bench/`. Evidence: this Task's comments.
        acceptance
            - The shard passes under its bound, or the bound is re-measured and recorded with its reason (a count may
              change only by a recorded re-measurement).
            - A per-firing cost that grew is found by profile and removed, or recorded as the cost of a rule.
            - Correctness: a corresponding fixture app exists that uses the feature (a whole app written the way a user
              would write it; see @convention.fixture-catalog for the apps and how to build them); a Story tests that
              fixture's feature, plus the Spec's Story of the wrong ending; a test runs the Story; type tests show the
              feature is typed fully and correctly, the way a user would expect (two-sided pins, no annotations needed
              in user code); each new runtime rule has a planted fault its own target tests catch. See the existing
              tests (`packages/flow-state-v3/tests/`, `fixtures/`) for how things are done now. Tests cover @spec.Host
              (R10, I18), @spec.Schedules (R17, I18); the Invariants its tests check after every step are @spec.Host
