               `tests/surface/doors.test.ts`, extended to walk Map and Set entries (scratch probe `deepface.ts`). Why
               the tests missed it: the test's walk and `seal` read the same own keys, so both miss what a collection
               holds. One rule is written twice; `core/frozen.ts#makeFreezer` is a third walker and does read entries.
               No requirement treated a collection's contents as part of the face. Fix with one walker for both, and
               with the Spec's sentence "what the core calls of it sits under the internals key", which the `Slot`
               closures make false.

            Routed:
            - Recognition is still split: `isMade`, which drops the Defect (`config.ts`, `construction.ts`);
              hand-written owner checks in `compile-body.ts:50` and `:164`; `tokens.get` for a guard's state token. So
              the planted `owner-compare-skipped` fault gets past the hostile table's ForeignToken row and is caught
              only by `context-slots.test.ts`. A throwing token as `default`, `target`, `send` or `guard` gives
              WrongKind, not one Defect. To @task.V3.T132: once intake lifts each value once, `madeOf` is its
              only reader.
            - Not built, and no Task row tracks them: `door(name, args, run)` (`src/entry.ts` still has four
              registration writers) and the construction-time `InvariantViolation` for an unregistered face function.
            - Sealing gaps for the fix above: a Map the app froze keeps its native writers (`sealOne` skips a
              non-extensible value); a non-empty typed array stays writable (`Result.try` swallows the freeze error).
            - To @task.V3.T132: `app()` with a null machine entry gives a Defect at `app`, not WrongKind at its path.
              Any function, an event token included, passes a guard or callback slot (`config.ts#Fn`; this predates
