        title       The typed machine (c): every hand-typed site swept, and a lint rule keeps them gone
        status      done
        priority    high
        parent      [@T142]
        blocked_by  [@T113]
        satisfies   [@plan.Machines.O1]
        description
            Every hand-typed site the survey found reads the derived types from @task.V3.T113 instead, each touched
            once, and a lint rule keeps them gone.

            **Why now:** split from @task.V3.T113 within the slice (the orchestrator's ruling, 2026-11-22). The sweep
            needs (a)'s types: `.Type`, `BodyTokens`, the face without the echo. That is why it is
            `blocked_by` @task.V3.T113.

            **Read first:** @task.V3.T113's comments (the inventory K1 to K6, and the review's added sites), @spec.Types
            (R1, I1, the lint rows), `tools/lint-bomb/FLOW-STATE.md`, and the Epic's "Lint-driven refactors" line.

            **Approach:**
            - The sites:
              - K1 to K6 of the inventory, plus the review's additions: `src/host/*.test.ts` (14 K1 and 5 K2 sites:
                `lanes.test.ts` 51, 80, 115, 159, 160, 199, 301, 307; `ledger.test.ts` 69, 83, 131, 142, 205, 211, 224;
                `runtime.test.ts` 56, 68; `life.test.ts` 44; `close.test.ts` 54);
              - K3 recounted (47 plus 22 lines);
              - `Held` at `tests/restore/schedule-declarations.test.ts:27`;
              - `TimerView`, `Outcome`, `Seen` and a second `Inputs` in `tests/turn/schedule-table.test.ts`
                and `schedule-keys-table.test.ts`;
              - `Confirmed` at `fixtures/services/shop/operations/writes.ts:30`;
              - the `Configure` payload at `fixtures/command-palette/stories/usual.ts:43-46`;
              - `Omit<typeof Head.M.n, …>` at `tests/actors/follow-ups.test.ts:28`.
              - the envelope `ReturnType<typeof Latest.E.Commit>` at `fixtures/lanes/stories/usual.ts:62`, and the five
                K4 `Memory` copies (re-review, @task.V3.T142).
            - Out of class:
              - anything inside `loose(...)` (`schedule-sequences.test.ts:182-204`, `schedule-records.test.ts:116-130`);
              - a derived alias;
              - an expected type in a type test;
              - the user's service port interfaces.
            - One place for each helper: one `byRepo` and one `fromAccount`.
            - Helpers rewritten the way a user writes them (the orchestrator, 2026-11-22). `palette.ts`'s `Picked<P>`,
              `Pick<P>` and the generic `known`, `enabled`, `hasPage` and `runs` exist only to share one handler body
              between Run (`event.key`) and Enter (the highlight). With `Reads` deferred, no handler-input type exists.
              So do not swap the hand-typed `{ memory; event }` for derived parts and keep the generics. Write plain
              functions of plain values (`known(memory, key)`, `commandOf(memory, key)`), with each handler passing its
              own key inline, so nothing is generic over the payload. Do the same for every fixture helper that is
              generic over a handler's input only to share a body. The user reads the fixture diff closely.
            - The lint rule `no-hand-typed-callback`, in `tools/flow-state-lint/rules/`, with its test of the shapes it
              must flag (K1 to K3) and must not (out of class, and a wrapper whose inner types are derived).
            - The plants: rename `Sync`'s `SyncRepo.repo`, `StandingOrder`'s memory `account` and the `transfer`
              commit's `receipt`. Each must break every helper that reads it.

            May change: `fixtures/`, `tests/`, `src/**/*.test.ts`, `tools/flow-state-lint/`, `vite.config.ts`,
            `package.json`. Evidence: this Task's comments.

            False pass: a lint rule that flags nothing and is never run → caught by its plants test and its registration
            in `tools/flow-state-lint/index.ts`, `vite.config.ts` and `lint:rules`. A sweep that swaps one hand type for
            another → caught by each kind's `rg` done-check.
        acceptance
            - Every site the survey and the review found reads the derived types (@spec.Types R1). Each kind's `rg`
              done-check (in this Task's comments) returns only its listed out-of-class lines; exactly one `const
              byRepo` and one `fromAccount` remain. `nub run check` shows each site compiles.
            - No fixture helper is generic over a handler's input only to share a body. `palette.ts` has no `Picked`, no
              `Pick`, and no generic `known`, `enabled`, `hasPage` or `runs`.
            - Three planted type changes (`SyncRepo.repo`, `StandingOrder`'s `account`, `transfer`'s `receipt`) are
              planted faults in `tests/faults.ts`, each caught by `nub run faults --only` with its name and breaking
              every helper that reads the field.
            - The lint rule `no-hand-typed-callback` refuses a hand-written event, memory or ending-value annotation
              where a derived type exists (`{ readonly event: {...} }`, `{ readonly memory: {...} }`, a typed `.from`
              mapper parameter). A wrapper whose inner types are derived is allowed. Its test plants each flagged and
              each allowed shape, and the rule is registered in `tools/flow-state-lint/index.ts`, `vite.config.ts`
              and `lint:rules`.
            - `nub run check` and the suite pass.
            - Fixtures first (@convention.fixtures): no new matrix cell. The 12 manifest apps
              (`fixtures/index.ts#manifests`) compile unchanged in behaviour against the derived types.
            - Correctness: a corresponding fixture app exists that uses the feature (a whole app written the way a user
              would write it; see @convention.fixture-catalog for the apps and how to build them); a Story tests that
              fixture's feature, plus the Spec's Story of the wrong ending; a test runs the Story; type tests show the
              feature is typed fully and correctly, the way a user would expect (two-sided pins, no annotations needed
              in user code); each new runtime rule has a planted fault its own target tests catch. See the existing
              tests (`packages/flow-state-v3/tests/`, `fixtures/`) for how things are done now. Tests cover @spec.Types
              (R1, I1); the Invariants its tests check after every step are @spec.Types (I1; tests T1, T3).
