    T145
        title       Planted faults: runtime rules only, judged by their targets
        status      done
        priority    high
        description
            Planted faults cover runtime rules only and are judged by their own target tests, so `nub run faults --only`
            stays fast and no fault duplicates what a two-sided type or lint test already proves.

            **Why now:** the user's ruling (2026-11-22, Q7 B; Epic, "Building a Task"): a fault's whole-suite fallback
            cost a full suite run per mis-aimed fault, and faults for type and lint rules repeat their own
            `@ts-expect-error` and must-flag tests. @task.V3.T144 also made the runner type-check its scratch copies
            only so type faults could be caught.

            **Read first:** code:tools/faults/run.ts, code:packages/flow-state-v3/tests/faults.ts and the per-area fault
            lists it gathers.

            **Approach:** the runner judges a named fault by its target files only and reports a miss as missed (no
            whole-suite run); the scratch copies drop the type-check T144 added; every fault whose target is a type or
            lint rule is deleted with its list entry, each named in a Task comment with the two-sided test that already
            covers it.

            May change: `tools/faults/`, `packages/flow-state-v3/tests/*faults*.ts`, the Epic's glossary line on
            planted faults.
        acceptance
            - `nub run faults --only <name>` never runs the whole suite: a fault its targets miss is reported missed (a
              runner test shows it).
            - The scratch copies run no type-check; no planted fault targets a type or lint rule (an `rg` over the fault
              lists shows none), and each deleted one names its covering two-sided test.
            - Every remaining runtime fault is caught by its own targets, checked with `--only` in groups, never the
              whole run.
            - `nub run check` at zero and the package suite pass.
